{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795291636,"nonce":"4AffZ5upErJeudTAK4Cvyw99vw0=","title":"hybrid: a long-loop function called repeatedly from compiled code stays at Cranelift after its OSR hand-off","message":"In the default hybrid mode a hot Haxe kernel runs at Cranelift speed for the whole run, about 3.7 times slower than the same code under LLVM and than hxcpp. The log says the LLVM promotion was deferred with `reason=handed-off` and never asked for again. Found while looking at a report that hl.NativeArray lags behind cpp.NativeArray and Array\u003cSingle\u003e in hxcpp; whether that report has this cause is not confirmed.\n\nReproducer (Haxe 4.3.6, -hl): a function that loops over 4096 Singles, `a[i] = a[i] * 0.999 + b[i]; sum += a[i]`, 3000 repetitions per call, timed as best of 5 calls through a closure: `time(\"x\", () -\u003e viaArray(a, b))`. One variant takes Array\u003cSingle\u003e, one takes hl.NativeArray\u003cSingle\u003e. A hxcpp build of the same source uses Array\u003cSingle\u003e and cpp.NativeArray.unsafeGet/unsafeSet. Source and bytecode: scratchpad/na/NABench.hx and na.hl of the session that filed this; the source is about 55 lines and easy to rewrite.\n\nns per element, Mac, v0.1.21 plus local commits, two runs agree to 0.05:\n\n    hxcpp                          Array\u003cSingle\u003e 0.98   cpp.NativeArray 0.97\n    ash default (hybrid)           Array\u003cSingle\u003e 3.6    hl.NativeArray 3.6\n    ash --jit-tier cranelift       3.6                  3.6\n    ash --jit-tier llvm            1.22                 0.96\n    ash default, ASH_OSR=off       1.23                 0.97\n\nWith --jit-log the default run shows, for the kernel function:\n\n    install findex=31 NABench.viaArray tier=cranelift\n    osr-transfer proposes findex=31 tier=llvm\n    defer findex=31 tier=llvm reason=handed-off\n\nand no LLVM install follows. With ASH_OSR=off the function gets its ordinary LLVM promote and runs at LLVM speed (the compile took 300 ms for viaArray).\n\nReading of the cause, from the code and the log, not yet proven: the postponement from e510c5f (1c52a4f) skips the ordinary promote when an OSR exit was published and the function was entered at most once from the interpreter, is not entered again, and is not named by several call sites. Here the function is entered once by the interpreter, then called five times from a closure in compiled code. That is one call site, outside a loop of its own, and compiled callers tick nothing, so the skipped promote is never requested again. The comment on that block already names this failure for functions called from compiled code; the guards cover direct call sites, not a call through a closure. The OSR exit itself did not help the measured pass: the first pass finished before the LLVM entry was ready.\n\nIn hybrid, a kernel that is called a few times and loops long is common: a physics step, a render pass, a benchmark body.\n\nOptions:\n- Keep the postponement, but record that a promote is owed and request it when the function is next entered, from any caller.\n- Run the postponed promote at low priority after the entry is published instead of skipping it.\n- Treat a function whose callers are closures or compiled code as called again.\n\nRelated: e510c5f (closed, introduced the postponement), c59a832 (closed, nested-loop frames), 5aaf58e (open, LLVM OSR entries take seconds on a physics program).\n\nOpen:\n- Not run on the NUC; the Mac was under load, though the 3.6 against 1.2 gap is far larger than the noise.\n- The reproducer from the hxn side for the hl.NativeArray report has been requested. If their gap is a different shape, for example NativeArray slower than Array\u003cSingle\u003e under LLVM, that is a second cause.","files":null}]}